Meeting Agenda – Phase 3
Project: Campus Residence Maintenance Reporting System (CRMRS)
Phase: 3 – Features, Scenarios, and User Stories
Duration: 60–90 minutes

1. Call to Order (5 min)
Welcome & roll call

Confirm roles (Product Owner, Scrum Master, Developers, etc.)

Review Phase 3 objectives from the project brief

2. Review of Phase 2 Outputs & Current Backlog (10 min)
Quick recap of Agile framework, sprints, and GitHub project board status

Identify any carry-over items affecting Phase 3

Confirm that the product vision is still aligned with the CRMRS problem statement (plumbing, electrical, carpentry, slow manual reporting)

3. Feature Identification (15 min)
Goal: Create features_list.md

Brainstorm features based on:

Problem statement (slow manual reporting, poor communication)

Proposed solution (digital platform, notifications, tracking)

Target users (students, maintenance staff, housing management)

Categorize features as:

Must have – e.g., submit complaint, category selection, status update

Should have – e.g., WhatsApp/email notification, feedback after resolution

Could have – e.g., photo upload, auto-assign technician, analytics dashboard

📌 Assign one person to document in docs/features/features_list.md

4. Personas & User Scenarios (15 min)
Goal: Create personas_and_scenarios.md

Define 2–3 personas:

Example 1: Student with urgent repair (e.g., no hot water)

Example 2: Maintenance staff managing many requests

Example 3: Residence manager tracking unresolved issues

For each persona, write one realistic scenario:

“Lebo (2nd year student) discovers a blocked sink at 9 PM. She opens the app, selects ‘Plumbing’, describes the issue, and submits. She expects a response within 2 hours.”

📌 Document in same folder

5. User Stories (15 min)
Goal: Create user_stories.md

Convert scenarios into user stories using the format:
As a [user role], I want to [goal] so that [benefit].

Examples for CRMRS:

As a student, I want to select a maintenance category so that my complaint reaches the right team.

As a maintenance staff member, I want to update the status of a complaint so that students know when to expect a fix.

As a residence manager, I want to see unresolved complaints so that I can follow up with maintenance teams.

Identify any epics (e.g., “notification system” may be broken into: email, SMS, in-app)

📌 Ensure every story links back to a persona/scenario

6. Requirements Mapping (10 min)
Goal: Create requirements_mapping.md

For the top 3–5 features, map to:

Functional requirements (what the system must do)

Non-functional requirements (performance, security, usability, availability)

Example:
Feature: Submit a complaint

Functional: User must enter residence, room number, description

Non-functional: Complaint submission must complete within 10 seconds on campus Wi-Fi

7. GitHub Project Board & Labeling (10 min)
Create docs/features/ folder in GitHub

Upload the 4 markdown files

Convert user stories and features into GitHub Issues

Apply labels: feature, user story, requirement

Move relevant items on the Project Board:
Backlog → To Do → In Progress → Review → Done

📌 Scrum Master ensures board reflects meeting decisions

